{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":288782,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"8bfc8137-3a50-43cf-93d2-5135d61da14b","total_cost_usd":1.349397,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68626,"cache_read_input_tokens":20654,"output_tokens":24064,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68626,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":24064,"cache_read_input_tokens":20654,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68626,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68626},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":51115,"outputTokens":17,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0512,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":24064,"cacheReadInputTokens":20654,"cacheCreationInputTokens":68626,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.298197,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"Six checks fail: the full hold is a fifth of the mass it is priced at, which drags the parachute claim down with it, the module cannot come home on one braking pass at any shield tier, fragile pieces are still crushable, and base_slots was moved off the GDD's 6 to make the hold mass come out.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"single_band_and_pieces_inside\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"There is exactly one orbital band and every catalog piece sits inside it; the params use only the sample keys bottom, middle, top.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"bands has length 1: 50,000-280,000 m. All 30 debris altitudes fall inside it — lowest torn_foil_blanket 52,500 m, highest armstrongs_module 277,000 m. Params sample keys: ablation.heat_index {bottom, middle, top}, ablation.cost_curve {bottom, middle, top}, ablation.optimal_skims {bottom, middle, top} — no other key appears anywhere in the params.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_matches_debris\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises: piece count, mean mass, fragile spawn share.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Thirds of the 230,000 m envelope are 50,000-126,667 / 126,667-203,333 / 203,333-280,000, matching the reported midpoints 88,333 / 165,000 / 241,667. Bottom: 11 pieces (claimed 11), sum 218.0 kg / 11 = 19.82 (claimed 19.82), fragile weight 3+2+2+2=9 over total weight 89 = 0.1011 (claimed 0.100, i.e. 9/90 — the designer's own weight total is one high; the error is 1.1% relative and nothing downstream reads it). Middle: 10 pieces (claimed 10), 529.0/10 = 52.90 (claimed 52.9), 12/69 = 0.1739 exact. Top: 9 pieces (claimed 9), 4449.0/9 = 494.33 (claimed 494.33), 10/40 = 0.2500 exact.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_mass_rises_with_altitude\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"The catalog's mean piece mass rises with altitude, third over third.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"19.82 kg -> 52.90 kg -> 494.33 kg, strictly rising. Excluding Armstrong's module from the top third the figure is (4449-3600)/8 = 106.1 kg, still a 2.0x step over the middle and 5.4x over the bottom, so the bet holds with or without the win-condition piece.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_with_altitude\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises with altitude, roughly 1 in 10 at the bottom to 1 in 4 at the top.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted: 9/89 = 0.101, 12/69 = 0.174, 10/40 = 0.250 — monotone, and the endpoints are 1-in-9.9 and 1-in-4.0 against the spec's 1-in-10 and 1-in-4. Counted rather than weighted it would be 4/11, 4/10, 4/9 = 0.36/0.40/0.44, nearly flat, so the gradient is genuinely carried by the weights.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"base_slots 7 against dry_mass_kg 650. The shipping slice (bottom two thirds, 21 pieces) has a mean piece mass of 747.0/21 = 35.6 kg, so a full hold is 7 x 35.6 = 249 kg = 0.38x dry mass — a total of 1.38x, not 2x. Against the bottom third alone (19.82 kg) it is 7 x 19.82 = 139 kg = 1.21x. The flight agrees and is worse: kg_per_slot 17.36, full_hold_kg 121.5, full_hold_mass_ratio 1.19. The Balancer's 742.7 kg figure used the top third's 106.1 kg ordinary mean, which is not the shipping slice the player fills a starter hold from. Undershoots doubling by 60-81%, far outside the 25% tolerance.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise the cargo mass the hold actually carries — more base_slots or a heavier representative piece mass — or cut dry_mass_kg; 650 kg of dry mass needs roughly 650 kg of cargo in 7 slots, i.e. ~93 kg per slot against the flown 17.36.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_cost_curve_optimum_in_range\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The skim cost curve's minimum equals optimal_skims at every sample, and the curve is what the stated model actually produces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Model recomputed from params.ablation (base 8.0, growth 1.75, coef 0.0009, exp 2, skim_peak 52, multiplier [1,0.48,0.45,0.44], heat_index 100/116.7/144.7). Bottom k=0: toll 8.0 + 0.0009*100^2 = 8.0+9.0 = 17.00 vs array 17.00. Bottom k=1: toll 8.0+14.0 = 22.0, skim 0.0009*52^2 = 2.434, entry 0.0009*(100*0.48)^2 = 2.074, total 26.507 vs array 26.51. Bottom k=3: 89.375+7.301+1.742 = 98.418 vs 98.42. Middle k=0: 8.0+0.0009*116.7^2 = 20.257 vs 20.26; k=1: 22.0+2.434+0.0009*56.016^2 = 27.258 vs 27.26. Top k=0: 8.0+0.0009*144.7^2 = 26.844 vs 26.84; k=1: 22.0+2.434+0.0009*69.456^2 = 28.775 vs 28.78. All eight recomputed points match within 0.03%. Minima: bottom 17.00 at k=0, middle 20.26 at k=0, top 26.84 at k=0, equal to optimal_skims {0,0,0}. balance_notes states this same model verbatim. The measured skim study agrees the argmin is at zero skims when entry depth is free.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"heavy_descent_requires_multi_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"With the commit floor in force, the endgame haul cannot survive a single committed plunge, can survive with a braking pass, and an empty ship and a full hold can still plunge from anywhere in the band.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The floor is real: commit_floor_m 8,000 against atmosphere_top_m 43,000 — 35,000 m below the interface, so entry depth is genuinely constrained. Judged on committed_descents only. (a) Plunge infeasible: endgame plunge_peak_heat 245.3 / 294.6 / 368.3 (bottom/middle/top), all above the 238 maxed tier and the 208 base — holds. (c) Light loads still plunge: empty 94.1 / 116.7 / 148.9 and full hold 102.8 / 127.2 / 162.0, all under the 208 base capacity — holds, with 22% margin at the worst cell. (b) FAILS: one skim gives the module 227.6 / 243.9 / 262.1, and the module hangs at 277,000 m, i.e. the top sample — 262.1 against 238 is 10% over, and against the 208 base it is 26% over. Every one of the twelve endgame rows reports survives:false and skim_saves_it:false. Only two skims (three passes) reach 231.3 at the top, which clears the 238 maxed tier by 2.8% and still fails the 208 base. The params' claimed 196.5 skimmed peak is not reproduced in any cell. Note that a one-skim finale is not reachable by ANY capacity that also forbids the plunge band-wide: it would need capacity > 262.1 while staying under the 245.3 bottom-sample plunge. A two-skim finale is satisfiable — the window (231.3, 245.3) is real, 5.7% wide, and the current 238 maxed tier already sits inside it.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise the base heat_capacity into the (231.3, 245.3) window so the finale is feasible at every tier rather than only the maxed one, and accept a two-skim finale — or cool the module's entry (lower cargo mass or a deeper commit_floor_m) until one skim lands it under 245.3.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_multiplier_matches_flight\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed skim_heat_multiplier matches the flown multiplier at the top sample, entry by entry, within 0.10.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Claimed [1, 0.48, 0.45, 0.44] against measured top [1, 0.439, 0.439, 0.439]: deltas 0.000, 0.041, 0.011, 0.001 — the largest is 0.041, under the 0.10 bar. (Recorded, not failed here: the same array is 0.48 against a measured 0.702 at the bottom sample, a delta of 0.222, and 0.524 at the middle — the quantity is band-dependent and the params carry a single vector.)\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_curve_shape_and_toll_growth\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"skim_heat_multiplier starts at 1.0, is non-increasing and saturates by its last two entries; cycle_toll_growth exceeds 1.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"[1, 0.48, 0.45, 0.44]: first entry exactly 1.0; differences -0.52, -0.03, -0.01, all non-positive; the last two are 0.01 apart, a 2.2% step against the 52% first step, so it flattens. The flight saturates harder still — the measured second and third skims move the peak by exactly zero at every sample and every entry depth scanned. cycle_toll_growth 1.75 > 1, giving tolls 8.0, 14.0, 24.5, 42.9 per cycle; six passes cost 8.0*(1.75^7-1)/0.75 = 514% of plate, so feathering is priced out rather than merely discouraged.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_speed_under_soft_threshold\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Full-hold descent speed under the canopy is below the 5 m/s soft-landing line but close enough to it that the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase (above about 3.5 m/s).\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Flown full-hold touchdown is 3.27 m/s at every sample (bottom, middle and top descents all report touchdown_ms 3.27, soft_landing true), 35% under the 5.0 m/s line and below the 3.5 m/s floor this check sets. Empty is 3.00 m/s, so the entire empty-to-full range spans 0.27 m/s and never approaches the threshold. The base 480 m2 canopy already lands every non-module load soft, so parachute tier 1 (700 m2, 1,000) and tier 2 (1,200 m2, 2,350) buy nothing the player can feel. The claimed 4.4 m/s would have been in range; it assumed a 1,392.7 kg hold against a flown 771.5 kg.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Shrink parachute_area_m2 below 480, or fix the hold mass upward — sqrt scaling means the canopy needs to see roughly 1.8x the flown loaded mass, or 0.55x the area, to put full-hold touchdown back near 4.4 m/s.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_speed_matches_flight\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed descent speed is what the stated canopy actually flies, measured independently.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"independent is true — parachute_area_m2 480 and parachute_drag_coefficient 1.8 are stated in the params, so the measurement is genuine and not solved backwards; that part of the rule is finally being tested. But claimed_full_hold_ms 4.4 against measured_full_hold_ms 3.27 is delta_ms 1.13, which is 25.7% of the claim and five times the 5% bar. The disagreement is entirely the hold mass: the flight massed 771.5 kg where the params assumed 1,392.7 kg, and sqrt(771.5/1392.7) * 4.4 = 3.28, reproducing the measurement to 0.3%.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Restate descent_speed_full_hold_ms from the mass the ship actually flies, or fix the full-hold mass first — the claim is arithmetically correct for a hold that does not exist.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_and_is_linear\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamps at exactly 50% of haul value, and can never go negative.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"free_radius_m 1200, curve 'linear', max_fee_fraction 0.5. Fee(d) = 0 for d <= 1200; for d > 1200 it rises linearly to 0.5 at the antipode, pi * 200,000 = 628,319 m, a slope of 0.5/627,119 = 7.97e-7 of haul value per metre. At d = 1200 the fee is 0.000; at 100,000 m it is 0.0787 of haul; the clamp binds at exactly 0.500 and no further. Expressed as a fraction of haul value it cannot be negative (d >= 0) and cannot touch banked cash.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap pieces near the floor of the band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"launch_cost 120. Gradient multiplier at altitude h is 1.0 + 4.5*(h-50,000)/230,000. crumpled_panel_stack at 56,000 m: 1 + 4.5*(6,000/230,000) = 1.117, small base 40 -> 44.7 each; three = 134.1 > 120, so the launch fee is 2.68 cheap pieces, inside the GDD's 'two to three'. Even the cheapest piece in the game, torn_foil_blanket at 52,500 m, prices at 40 * 1.049 = 41.96 before its 3x fragile premium, and three of those are 125.9 > 120.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"towing_never_shears_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state towing tension stays below magnet_hold_force_n with margin at the heaviest legal load.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Heaviest hand-tetherable piece is impact_pitted_engine_bell at 122 kg (severed_boom_mast at 165 kg is oversized and not hand-tetherable). Two of them: a = 240 / (110 + 244) = 0.678 m/s2, per-cable tension = 122 * 0.678 = 82.7 N, total 165.4 N — against magnet_hold_force_n 900 that is a 10.9x per-cable margin, 5.4x on the total. Worst case in the game, Armstrong's module at 3,600 kg: a = 240/3,710 = 0.0647 m/s2, tension 232.9 N, still a 3.9x margin. Shear can only fire on latch or swing jerk, both left at coefficient 1.0.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter reintroduces thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The only thrust quantities in the params are flight.thrust_n (28,500, upgraded to 33,000 and 38,000) and flight.rcs_thrust_n (1,200), both attached to the thruster/tank stack that staging jettisons, plus eva.jetpack_thrust_n 240 which belongs to the astronaut and not the ship. No upgrade, no ablation entry and no landing entry grants thrust, delta-v or a restart to the staged configuration; the twelve purchases modify range, capacity, thrust-before-staging, area, slots and heat capacity only.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable_and_premium_holds\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile pieces cannot be crushed at any compactor tier present in the params, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of the same class at a similar altitude.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The premium half holds: fragile_value_premium 3 means cracked_optics_barrel at 138,000 m prices at 40 * (1+4.5*88,000/230,000) * 3 = 40 * 2.722 * 3 = 326.6 against a solid small piece at the same altitude at 108.9 — worth 3.0 solid pieces of its class, which is 'several'. The crushability half fails: params carry cargo.compactor_tier = 1, a live tier, while the catalog's size_classes block still assigns slots_crushed to every class (small 1, medium 2, oversized 6). Nothing in either artifact exempts a fragile piece, so all 12 fragile entries — torn_foil_blanket, cracked_solar_array, shredded_antenna_mesh, delaminated_radiator_fin, cracked_optics_barrel, shattered_star_tracker, cracked_ablative_nose_cone, split_avionics_rack, cracked_deep_space_dish, fused_ion_grid_assembly, shattered_cryo_dewar, cracked_command_module_hatch — inherit a crushed slot count from their class and are crushable at tier 1. The designer's prose says the optics barrel 'cannot be crushed at any tier', but that is a note on one piece, not a field.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Drop slots_crushed/slots_uncrushed from size_classes so every piece is one slot as GDD 4.3 states, or add an explicit uncrushable field the fragile flag sets.\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable is false, against true for small and medium. The three oversized pieces — collapsed_dish_frame (86,500 m, 48 kg), buckled_pressure_bulkhead (160,000 m, 96 kg), severed_boom_mast (234,000 m, 165 kg) — therefore cannot be tethered by hand, one per third, and the first is met inside the shipping slice at 86,500 m so the limit is learned early.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve purchases: six parts at two tiers each, tier 2 dearer than tier 1 for the same part, each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries across exactly 6 parts, 2 tiers each. Costs tier1 -> tier2: hand_magnet 680 -> 1,500; fuel_tank 750 -> 1,600; thruster 850 -> 1,900; parachute 1,000 -> 2,350; storage 1,100 -> 2,500; heat_shield 1,250 -> 2,950 — every tier 2 dearer, total ladder 18,430. Every stat improves monotonically from its base: magnet_range_screens 0.125/0.1875/0.25, fuel_capacity_kg 420/560/700, thrust_n 28,500/33,000/38,000, parachute_area_m2 480/700/1,200, base_slots 7/10/13, heat_capacity 208/222/238.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"launch_survives_itself\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The climb's peak heat stays under reentry.heat_capacity.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The hottest recorded climb is the bottom-sample ascent at climb_peak_heat 53.3 against heat_capacity 208 — 25.6% of the base bar, and 22.4% of the 238 maxed tier, with climb_survivable true. That is close to the GDD's stated 47.5 and is a visible but safe first lesson. The middle and top ascents log climb_peak_heat 0 because they never got far enough to heat the ship ('crashed on ascent'), so 53.3 is the only real datum and it clears by 3.9x.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_reachable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"The base ship reaches the band floor, by arc or by orbit, and the bottom two thirds each carry at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Judged at the floor on a ballistic arc, as the rule requires. Bottom sample: reached true, route 'arc', arc_apex_m 72,880 against a band floor of 50,000 m and a sample altitude of 65,000 m, arc_eva_window_s 118.9 — above the GDD's 97 s, so there is time to tether and stow. The route is the arc, not orbit: orbit_reached false, 'out of fuel before circularising'. Catalog population — bottom third: fragile torn_foil_blanket, cracked_solar_array, shredded_antenna_mesh, delaminated_radiator_fin; classes small, medium and oversized all present. Middle third: fragile cracked_optics_barrel, shattered_star_tracker, cracked_ablative_nose_cone, split_avionics_rack; classes small, medium and oversized all present. Recorded but not failed here, because the rule is judged at the floor: the arc arrives with 5.72 kg of fuel, a 1.4% margin against the GDD's stated 29.5%, and the middle sample at 115,000 m — inside the slice — reports 'crashed on ascent' with an apex of 84,977 m. See observations.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_target_moved_to_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule passes only because a number the design document fixes in prose was moved.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.base_slots is 7. The GDD's §2.2 walkthrough puts 'Cargo reads 4 of 6' on screen and the appendix poses its open question against 2 tethers and 6 slots, so the fixed value is 6. eva.base_tether_slots is 2 and matches. The Balancer states the reason for the move outright: 'I set base_slots to 7 because that is what makes the reentry table's 1,398 kg and the Researcher's 285 kg/m2 come out right' — the target was widened to hit the arrow. It did not even work: the flown full hold is 121.5 kg at 17.36 kg per slot, a 1.19x mass ratio, so full_hold_doubles_mass fails anyway and the sixth slot was spent for nothing. At 6 slots the same catalog gives 6 * 17.36 = 104 kg, 1.16x.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Return cargo.base_slots to the GDD's 6 and buy the hold-mass doubling from the piece masses or dry_mass_kg instead.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"Greed costs nothing measurable below the module. Across all three samples an empty ship lands at 3.00 m/s and a full hold at 3.27 m/s, single-pass peak heat moves from 65-71 to 71-77, and plate cost from 11.8% to 12.5% — same pass count, same soft landing, same everything. The hold-mass scale in §2.4 has no amber or red band to paint, so the trips-vs-greed decision the EVA loop is built on has no consequence attached to it.\",\n    \"The base ship arrives at the band floor with 5.72 kg of fuel, a 1.4% margin, and both higher samples crash on ascent at apex 84,977 m and 89,362 m. Combined with GDD 4.5's note that apex barely moves across the entire shop (56.2 -> 57.0 km, thrust-limited not fuel-limited), the upper half of the envelope — including the middle third of the shipping slice and the module at 277,000 m — may not be reachable by any purchasable ship. That is a progression question the audit's rules cannot fail but a human should answer before flying these numbers.\",\n    \"skim_heat_multiplier is a single vector for a quantity the flight measures as band-dependent: 0.702 at the bottom, 0.524 at the middle, 0.439 at the top. It passes the top-sample check, which is where the module is, but every bottom-sample entry in cost_curve is priced roughly 46% optimistic as a result. The measured saturation is exact — the second and third skims move the peak by zero — so the array's shape is right and only its level is band-blind.\",\n    \"The unstaged braking pass peaks at 424 bar units from the bottom sample against a 208-238 capacity ladder, and 88 from the middle. The staged-versus-unstaged choice §2.2 describes as real is a certain kill low in the band and nearly free in the middle. If the choice is meant to survive, unstaged_heat_multiplier (currently 3) is the lever, not the capacity.\",\n    \"Even in the configuration where the finale works — maxed 238 tier, two skims, 231.3 peak — the margin is 2.8%, and the toll for three heat cycles is 8.0 + 14.0 + 24.5 = 46.5% of plate before the entry's own heat cost. The endgame flight has no room for a mistake and no room for a third skim.\",\n    \"Two catalog entries price things the player cannot have. The three oversized pieces (48, 96 and 165 kg) are hand_tetherable false and GDD 4.3 cut the crane magnet, so economy.size_class_base_value.oversized = 320 values an unreachable class; and armstrongs_module carries spawn_weight 1, which places the win condition inside the ordinary weighted spawn pool rather than fixing it at 277,000 m as a one-off objective.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":176174,"ttft_stream_ms":1922,"time_to_request_ms":318,"type":"result","duration_ms":286788,"uuid":"1d3daccb-1a11-4f40-a0be-92b19941da00"}
